WEBVTT

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.775
Documentation works best when each document answers a

00:00:02.833 --> 00:00:03.669
clear question.

00:00:04.365 --> 00:00:07.790
A README is the front door: what is this project and where

00:00:07.814 --> 00:00:08.522
do I start?

00:00:09.218 --> 00:00:13.444
Session instructions, often named AGENTS.md, explain

00:00:13.526 --> 00:00:14.930
applicable working rules.

00:00:15.627 --> 00:00:18.529
A decision ledger says which important choices were made

00:00:18.553 --> 00:00:19.145
and why.

00:00:19.586 --> 00:00:22.326
An architecture document explains how parts fit together.

00:00:22.651 --> 00:00:25.925
A workflow document explains behavior over time, including

00:00:25.971 --> 00:00:26.447
failure.

00:00:26.958 --> 00:00:29.431
These documents serve different readers and should not

00:00:29.489 --> 00:00:31.033
silently contradict one another.

00:00:31.325 --> 00:00:34.019
The architecture shows responsibility and structure.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.478
The build specification states exactly what behavior must

00:00:37.525 --> 00:00:40.845
be delivered, including validation, boundaries, and

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.053
acceptance criteria.

00:00:42.656 --> 00:00:45.825
The build plan arranges work into an order: what depends

00:00:45.884 --> 00:00:49.529
on what, who owns each part, and how completion will be

00:00:49.564 --> 00:00:50.028
checked.

00:00:50.632 --> 00:00:53.488
For our tracker, the architecture says a service validates

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:54.603
and stores an inquiry.

00:00:54.986 --> 00:00:57.830
The spec defines the required fields and what the user

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:58.689
sees on error.

00:00:59.072 --> 00:01:02.300
The plan says which part to implement and verify first.

00:01:02.596 --> 00:01:05.893
A progress log tells us what happened recently, what is

00:01:05.951 --> 00:01:08.064
underway, and what comes next.

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:11.582
A changelog records meaningful changes over time.

00:01:11.965 --> 00:01:14.937
An evidence record captures what was actually checked,

00:01:15.262 --> 00:01:16.806
with the version and result.

00:01:17.248 --> 00:01:20.684
A runbook explains how to operate, inspect, and recover

00:01:20.742 --> 00:01:21.369
the system.

00:01:21.973 --> 00:01:25.769
A handoff is a concise restart point: the current state,

00:01:25.851 --> 00:01:30.216
known gaps, important decisions, exact blockers, and next

00:01:30.251 --> 00:01:30.994
safe action.

00:01:31.598 --> 00:01:34.210
A log is not proof by itself unless it points to

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:35.301
appropriate evidence.

00:01:35.649 --> 00:01:37.379
Documents have different authority.

00:01:37.704 --> 00:01:41.338
A more recent, explicitly approved narrow specification

00:01:41.616 --> 00:01:44.971
can supersede an older requirement where they conflict;

00:01:45.575 --> 00:01:47.909
other older requirements may still apply.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.275
A progress report can describe what someone believes is

00:01:51.345 --> 00:01:55.513
done, but it cannot turn an untested feature into verified

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:56.151
behavior.

00:01:56.755 --> 00:01:59.855
A kickoff can authorize an agent to act within its stated

00:01:59.913 --> 00:02:00.401
scope.

00:02:00.842 --> 00:02:04.116
When instructions conflict, ask the agent to identify the

00:02:04.186 --> 00:02:07.611
actual source and the precise conflict rather than quietly

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.457
choose whichever text is convenient.

00:02:09.814 --> 00:02:12.496
Not every tiny project needs a separate file for every

00:02:12.554 --> 00:02:13.111
concept.

00:02:13.552 --> 00:02:15.572
The important part is that an agent can find the

00:02:15.607 --> 00:02:20.158
decisions, scope, tests, operational steps, and current

00:02:20.205 --> 00:02:20.646
state.

00:02:21.087 --> 00:02:24.640
A small project might combine sections; a larger one may

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:25.453
separate them.

00:02:26.265 --> 00:02:29.133
Use the simplest organization that keeps responsibilities

00:02:29.179 --> 00:02:29.621
clear.

00:02:29.946 --> 00:02:32.604
A naming convention helps, but a filename does not

00:02:32.651 --> 00:02:35.228
guarantee that a document is current or authoritative.

00:02:35.669 --> 00:02:37.121
Read its content and date.

00:02:37.425 --> 00:02:40.943
Imagine the tracker changes from three statuses to two.

00:02:41.384 --> 00:02:44.774
Update the relevant decision and spec, implement the

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.897
change when authorized, record what changed in the

00:02:47.932 --> 00:02:52.088
changelog, record test results in evidence, and refresh

00:02:52.169 --> 00:02:53.644
progress and handoff.

00:02:54.154 --> 00:02:56.651
The architecture may not need to change if component

00:02:56.685 --> 00:02:58.288
responsibilities remain the same.

00:02:58.613 --> 00:03:02.084
This is why each document has one job: we can update the

00:03:02.119 --> 00:03:04.789
places whose truth actually changed without rewriting

00:03:04.836 --> 00:03:05.474
every page.

00:03:05.825 --> 00:03:09.110
A tiny project might start with a README, a decisions

00:03:09.157 --> 00:03:12.965
section, a combined architecture and specification, a

00:03:13.058 --> 00:03:14.567
plan, and a handoff.

00:03:15.171 --> 00:03:18.131
As the project grows, separate documents when readers need

00:03:18.178 --> 00:03:20.013
different levels of detail or authority.

00:03:20.454 --> 00:03:23.147
The aim is not to create files for their own sake.

00:03:23.531 --> 00:03:25.794
Ask whether a new contributor can find the current

00:03:25.841 --> 00:03:29.382
behavior, the reason behind a decision, what remains to

00:03:29.452 --> 00:03:31.402
build, and how to verify a change.

00:03:31.843 --> 00:03:34.653
If those answers are clear, the structure is doing its

00:03:34.699 --> 00:03:35.152
job.

00:03:35.523 --> 00:03:38.159
Suppose an older build spec says the owner can choose

00:03:38.182 --> 00:03:39.784
among three inquiry statuses.

00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:42.803
A later approved narrow spec says to use only New and

00:03:42.861 --> 00:03:43.325
Handled.

00:03:43.766 --> 00:03:46.111
The later decision controls that conflict.

00:03:46.494 --> 00:03:49.490
An older privacy rule that does not conflict can still

00:03:49.525 --> 00:03:50.047
apply.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.240
A helpful agent points to both documents and states

00:03:53.298 --> 00:03:55.005
exactly which clause changed.

00:03:55.446 --> 00:03:58.824
It does not discard every older requirement merely because

00:03:58.859 --> 00:04:00.206
a newer document exists.

00:04:00.525 --> 00:04:04.263
A progress log may say "validation implementation is

00:04:04.321 --> 00:04:08.501
underway; failure-path test remains." A changelog may

00:04:08.571 --> 00:04:11.717
later say "added validation and clear missing-field

00:04:11.763 --> 00:04:16.001
errors." Evidence records the actual check, revision, and

00:04:16.071 --> 00:04:16.639
result.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:19.913
These entries answer different questions and can refer to

00:04:19.994 --> 00:04:20.610
one another.

00:04:20.993 --> 00:04:23.942
They should not claim a deployment or live result until it

00:04:23.988 --> 00:04:25.300
has actually occurred.

00:04:25.997 --> 00:04:28.946
Keeping these roles distinct lets a reviewer find the

00:04:28.992 --> 00:04:31.825
current blocker without reading the entire project

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:32.394
history.

00:04:32.725 --> 00:04:35.384
At the end of a session, tell the next reader the branch

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.470
or revision, current work state, authoritative documents,

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:44.079
tests run, unverified areas, running services if relevant,

00:04:44.323 --> 00:04:46.320
and exact next safe step.

00:04:47.017 --> 00:04:49.525
Mention any uncertain external effect that needs

00:04:49.571 --> 00:04:50.512
reconciliation.

00:04:50.953 --> 00:04:53.739
If the previous session ended after a provider request was

00:04:53.786 --> 00:04:57.083
sent, that uncertainty matters more than a long narrative

00:04:57.118 --> 00:04:58.882
about how hard the task was.

00:04:59.393 --> 00:05:02.156
A handoff is a practical starting point, not a

00:05:02.226 --> 00:05:03.677
congratulatory summary.

00:05:03.925 --> 00:05:06.827
Imagine the tracker owner changes the required contact

00:05:06.874 --> 00:05:07.280
method.

00:05:07.721 --> 00:05:10.090
First record the decision and update the approved

00:05:10.113 --> 00:05:11.053
specification.

00:05:11.495 --> 00:05:14.118
Then adapt the plan and implement within the granted

00:05:14.165 --> 00:05:14.652
scope.

00:05:15.094 --> 00:05:18.809
Add or update tests for both accepted and rejected inputs.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.223
Record the check and revision in evidence, update

00:05:22.269 --> 00:05:25.427
progress, and mention the material behavior change in the

00:05:25.462 --> 00:05:26.182
changelog.

00:05:26.785 --> 00:05:29.978
If the change affects data flow or responsibilities,

00:05:30.222 --> 00:05:31.836
update the architecture too.

00:05:32.277 --> 00:05:35.028
If it does not, leave unrelated sections alone.

00:05:35.325 --> 00:05:38.529
If the README says a feature is live while the handoff

00:05:38.587 --> 00:05:41.919
says deployment is pending, do not pick one sentence

00:05:41.954 --> 00:05:43.394
because it sounds more confident.

00:05:43.835 --> 00:05:46.505
Inspect dated evidence and current state where authorized.

00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:49.721
Report the contradiction and correct the stale description

00:05:49.767 --> 00:05:50.894
when the task permits.

00:05:51.404 --> 00:05:55.154
If the evidence is insufficient, label the status unknown.

00:05:55.758 --> 00:05:58.289
Documents are useful because they make information

00:05:58.347 --> 00:06:01.760
inspectable; they cannot substitute for the observation

00:06:01.795 --> 00:06:02.898
they claim to represent.

00:06:03.214 --> 00:06:05.977
Give a partner only your document map and a short project

00:06:06.012 --> 00:06:06.488
folder.

00:06:06.929 --> 00:06:10.122
Ask where they would find the original goal, an approved

00:06:10.145 --> 00:06:13.547
decision, a failed check, and the next safe action.

00:06:14.151 --> 00:06:17.552
If they cannot answer, the information may be missing,

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:20.385
hidden, or duplicated inconsistently.

00:06:21.082 --> 00:06:22.730
Revise the map and try again.

00:06:23.055 --> 00:06:26.538
In a real project, a fresh agent can do a similar review,

00:06:26.747 --> 00:06:29.952
but label that as an agent review rather than claiming a

00:06:30.021 --> 00:06:31.681
human learner tested the material.

00:06:32.378 --> 00:06:35.152
The goal is that a newcomer can navigate without your oral

00:06:35.222 --> 00:06:35.954
explanation.
